amdxdna: remap cached control-code BOs after deferred materialize

Windows instruction BOs start as deferred host storage. The first submit
materializes them into a KMT mapping and frees that temporary storage, so a
`ctrl_code_mapped_ptr` captured at `make_npu_cmd` time then dangles. In-place
chain-cache rewrites memcpy into the freed storage while Path-B publication
copies the live BO -- still the first-token image.

This is a real in-place rewrite bug, not the qwen3.5 GDN decode mismatch.
That RTP program has an empty patch table and never entered this PARTIAL_ELF
chain rewrite (see patch 0010). Applying 0009 left the GDN hash unchanged.

Always re-map before rewriting control code, treat TXN byte changes as
`code_changed` in the PARTIAL_ELF chain rewrite, and publish the rewritten
Lock2 range (`SyncBuffer`) even on Path-B. A process write barrier does not
evict dirty CPU cache lines from that mapping, so aperture publication can
otherwise restage the pre-rewrite image.
